<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fayzulla Khodzhayev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a>–<a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Fayzulla Khojaev</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fayzulla Khodzhayev was the son of a wealthy Bukharan merchant and a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/young-bukharans">Young Bukharan</a> reform movement. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> in 1920 he headed the government of the Bukharan People's Soviet Republic, and from 1925 he chaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-peoples-commissars">Council of People's Commissars</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> SSR. Accused of bourgeois nationalism, he was tried with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-bukharin">Nikolai Bukharin</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> show trial of 1938 and shot.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fayzulla-khodzhayev.jpg" alt="Fayzulla Khodzhayev" width="320" height="486" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Youth_Fayzulla_Xo%27jayev.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fayzulla Xoʻjayev (Fayzulla Ubaydulloyevich Xoʻjayev) was born in 1896 into a family of wealthy traders in the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Bukhara. His father sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1907, where he came to see the gap between European society and technology and the tradition-bound ways of his homeland. His father died in 1912. In 1916 he joined the Jadid movement of reformers and, with his father's fortune, founded the Young Bukharan Party.</p><p>In March 1918, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> had established Soviet rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a>, he led an attempt to form a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/young-bukharans">Young Bukharan</a> government with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> as a figurehead. For a few days it seemed to succeed, but in his later account he admitted that he and his fellow revolutionaries had been gullible and had underestimated the clergy and the forces loyal to the old regime. Thousands of Young Bukharan supporters were killed in the reaction. He escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> and was sentenced to death in his absence, returning only after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> overthrew the Emir's administration on 2 September 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After joining the Russian Communist Party in mid-1920, Xoʻjayev was appointed Chairman of the Council of People's Nazirs of the Bukharan People's Soviet Republic in September 1920. A Basmachi attempt on his life failed on 7 July 1922. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> temporarily recognised the republic as independent and sent an ambassador and advisers. The Soviet diplomat Alexander Barmine remembered him as small, wiry and full of energy, called the Lenin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a>.</p><p>When Soviet Central Asia was reorganised, Xoʻjayev became Chair of the Revolutionary Committee of the Uzbek SSR on 5 December 1924 and Chair of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-peoples-commissars">Council of People's Commissars</a> on 17 February 1925. He opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>'s heavy-handed control, particularly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> monoculture. From 1929 his influence was eclipsed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akmal-ikramov">Akmal Ikramov</a>, the First Secretary of the Uzbek party, and he was never elected to the Central Committee of the Soviet party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In June 1937 Xoʻjayev was absent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> party's Seventh Congress, was removed from office on 27 June and was arrested by 9 July. In March 1938 he and Ikramov were arraigned at the Trial of the Twenty-One in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, each accusing the other of lying. He confessed to membership of a secret pan-Turkic society, Milli Ittikhad, and to opposing the division of Turkestan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> policy. He was sentenced to death, executed on 13 March 1938 and buried at Kommunarka. He was rehabilitated officially in 1966 and remains a controversial figure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, with few monuments; his father's house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> is a museum styled as the House of a Wealthy Local Merchant.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fayzulla Xoʻjayev” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Led by <b>Fayzulla Khodzhayev</b> as chairman of the council of people's nazirs, the republic carried out land and educational reforms but remained dependent on Soviet troops and money.</blockquote><a href="/state/bukharan-peoples-soviet-republic">Bukharan People's Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic was transformed by collectivisation, the Latin and then Cyrillic scripts, the hujum campaign against veiling, and purges that killed leaders like <b>Fayzulla Khodzhayev</b> and Akmal Ikramov.</blockquote><a href="/state/uzbek-ssr">Uzbek SSR</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.78,64.43&amp;year=1923" aria-label="Bukhara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6138 740 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Fayzulla Khodzhayev"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6588 1010h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6588 1010h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.78,64.43&amp;year=1923">What was here in 1923? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
